:: LearnAboutStones [childhood]
# Earth's Treasures

Your grandfather examines the collection of stones you've gathered, his weathered hands turning each one carefully. "You have found some useful stones here," he says with approval.

He picks up a dark, glassy stone. "This is flint," he explains. "It is one of our most valuable resources. When struck properly, it creates sharp edges for cutting tools and sparks for making fire."

Next, he shows you a reddish stone with bands of color. "We crush this to make paint for our bodies and for marking important objects. The color comes from the earth's blood."

A smooth, round river stone catches his attention. "These make excellent hammers and grinding tools. The river has done the work of shaping them for us."

Your grandfather explains how different stones have different properties - some are hard enough to shape other stones, some can hold heat from a fire, and others might contain minerals that give them special colors or properties.

"Our ancestors learned which stones were useful through many generations of observation," he tells you. "This knowledge is part of what makes us human."

